sections.summaryEn:
ANVISA also grants notification or registration for families, systems and kits. A family is a grouping where each product has similar technical characteristics in:
(a) indication / purpose of use;
(b) operation and action;
(c) technology;
(d) content or composition, where applicable;
(e) precautions, restrictions, warnings and special care. Grouping rules follow a specific regulation.

The dossier contains label and IFU models (Art. 57 V), in Portuguese. The label model contains:
(I) legal manufacturer name and address, preceded by 'fabricante' or an equivalent symbol;
(II) name and address of the notification/registration holder;
(III) information to identify the device and the package contents;
(IV) where applicable the word 'Estéril' and the sterilization method;
(V) lot code preceded by 'Lote', or serial number;
(VI) as applicable, manufacture date and expiry date or use-before date;
(VII) where applicable, single-use indication;
(VIII) specific storage, conservation and handling conditions;
(IX) special operating/use instructions;
(X) all warnings and precautions;
(XI) name of the legally qualified technical manager;
(XII) notification/registration number preceded by the ANVISA acronym.

Notified or registered equipment must carry an indelible plate showing:
(I) commercial name with the model where applicable;
(II) legal manufacturer name or brand;
(III) ANVISA notification or registration number;
(IV) serial number or another identifier enabling traceability. For very small and/or implantable equipment, a manufacturer or brand inscription and traceability elements are required. For systems, all components must be identified as part of their system. Single-use non-implantable equipment is excluded.

Devices and IVDs must:
(I) achieve the performance intended by the manufacturer;
(II) be designed and manufactured to be suitable for the intended purpose under the established conditions of use;
(III) be safe;
(IV) work as intended;
(V) have risks that are acceptable compared with the benefits to the patient;
(VI) not compromise the clinical condition or the safety of patients or other persons.

In risk management, manufacturers must:
(I) establish and document a risk management plan for each device and IVD;
(II) identify and analyse the known and foreseeable hazards;
(III) estimate and evaluate the associated risks occurring during intended use and reasonably foreseeable misuse;
(IV) eliminate or control those risks per Arts. 10 and 11;
(V) assess the impact of production and post-production information on overall risk, benefit-risk determination and risk acceptability, including previously unknown hazards or hazardous situations, acceptability of estimated risks and changes in the state of the art;
(VI) based on that assessment, if necessary, change the control measures per Arts. 9 and 10.

Manufacturers must select the most suitable solutions in this order of priority:
(I) eliminate or adequately reduce risks through safe design and manufacture;
(II) as appropriate, adopt adequate protective measures, including alarms if necessary, for risks that cannot be eliminated;
(III) provide safety information — warnings, precautions and contraindications — and, as appropriate, training to users.

In eliminating or reducing risks related to use, the manufacturer must:
(I) adequately reduce risks related to the characteristics of the device and IVD and to their intended use environment;
(II) consider the technical knowledge, experience, education, training and use environment and, as the case may be, the medical and physical conditions of the intended users.

Devices and IVDs must have acceptable stability during:
(I) the shelf-life period;
(II) the time of use after opening, including after installation on the instrument in the case of IVDs;
(III) transport or shipping, including samples in the case of IVDs.

The clinical evaluation must analyse clinical data with scientific validity to prove a favourable benefit-risk relation of the device or IVD through one or more of:
(I) clinical evaluation report(s) with a critical analysis of relevant scientific literature whose results show an acceptable level of evidence in the hierarchy of evidence-based medicine;
(II) clinical investigation report(s) (for IVDs, clinical performance evaluation reports).

Reports of clinical investigations conducted specifically with the device to be regularised with ANVISA must be presented as proof of safety and efficacy in these situations:
(I) devices with innovations in design, raw material, indication of use, among others, regardless of risk class;
(II) Class III and IV devices that, owing to their unique nature and performance closely tied to material design and manufacturing process, require verification of safety and efficacy using clinical data specific to the device under evaluation.

Devices and IVDs and their manufacturing processes must be conceived to eliminate or adequately reduce the risk of infection of users and all other people who may come into contact with the device. The design must:
(I) allow easy and safe handling;
(II) adequately reduce any microbial release from the device or IVD and/or microbial exposure during use;
(III) prevent microbial contamination of the device and IVD or its contents;
(IV) adequately reduce the risks of unintended exposure.

Devices and IVDs supplied non-sterile and intended to be sterilised before use must:
(I) have a packaging system that minimises the risk of microbial contamination and is suitable for the sterilisation method indicated by the manufacturer;
(II) have the sterilisation method indicated by the manufacturer validated.

Devices and IVDs must be designed and manufactured considering the intended environment and conditions of use, to eliminate or adequately reduce:
(I) risks of injury to users or others related to physical, ergonomic or usability features;
(II) risks of user error from user-interface design, ergonomic or usability features and the intended use environment;
(III) risks associated with reasonably foreseeable external influences or environmental conditions such as magnetic fields, external electrical and electromagnetic effects, electrostatic discharge, radiation associated with diagnostic or therapeutic procedures, pressure, humidity, temperature and/or variations in pressure and acceleration;
(IV) risks associated with use when in contact with materials, liquids and substances, including gases, to which they are exposed under intended conditions of use;
(V) risks of possible negative interaction between the software and the IT environment in which it operates and interacts;
(VI) risks to the environment from accidental release of substances during use;
(VII) the risk of wrong identification of samples or data and of wrong results arising from confusion of colour and/or numerical coding of sample containers, removable parts and/or accessories used to perform the intended analysis, test or assay, and other situations;
(VIII) risks of interference with other devices or IVDs normally used in diagnosis, monitoring or treatment.

Devices and IVDs must be designed and manufactured to adequately reduce:
(I) risks from vibrations they produce, considering technological progress and the means available to limit vibrations, particularly at the source, unless vibrations are part of the specified performance;
(II) risks from emitted noise, on the same basis;
(III) the risk of failure of any part of the device intended to be connected or reconnected before or during use.

Devices and IVDs must be designed and manufactured to adequately reduce:
(I) risks of electromagnetic interference that may impair the operation of any device or equipment installed in the intended environment;
(II) the risk of accidental electric shock to the user or any other person, both in normal use and in a single fault condition, provided the devices are installed and maintained as indicated by the manufacturer.

Devices and IVDs for lay use must be designed and manufactured to:
(I) ensure that the user can use them safely and accurately per the instructions for use;
(II) adequately reduce the risk of user error in handling the device or IVD and, if applicable, in interpreting results. When the risks associated with interpreting the instructions for use cannot be reduced to acceptable levels, these risks may be mitigated by training.

Devices and IVDs for lay use must, when appropriate, include means by which users:
(I) can verify, at the time of use, that the device or IVD performs as intended by the manufacturer;
(II) are warned if the device or IVD does not work as intended or does not provide a valid result.

For devices and IVDs containing non-viable (or rendered non-viable) tissues, cells or substances of animal, plant or bacterial origin or their derivatives:
(I) as the case may be, taking the animal species into account, tissues and cells of animal origin or their derivatives must come from animals subjected to veterinary controls adapted to the intended use of the tissues;
(II) sourcing, processing, preservation, testing and handling of tissues, cells and substances of animal origin or their derivatives must be done so as to ensure the safety of patients, users and, as the case may be, other persons. Safety with regard to viruses and other transmissible agents must be addressed by implementing up-to-date, validated methods of elimination or inactivation during manufacturing, unless their use would cause unacceptable deterioration that impairs the device or IVD.

Analytical performance includes, among others:
(I) traceability of calibrators and controls;
(II) measurement accuracy (trueness and precision);
(III) analytical sensitivity / limit of detection;
(IV) analytical specificity;
(V) measuring interval / range; and
(VI) sample stability.

IVD performance characteristics must be evaluated according to the intended purpose, which must include:
(I) intended user: lay or professional;
(II) intended use environment: patient's home, emergency units, ambulances, healthcare centres, laboratory;
(III) target populations: paediatric, adult, pregnant women, people with signs and symptoms of a specific disease, patients in differential diagnosis, blood donors, among others.

To petition notification the applicant pays the fee and files:
(I) the notification form completed, available on the ANVISA portal;
(II) for imported devices, a declaration from the legal manufacturer, consularized or apostilled, in Portuguese, English or Spanish or with a sworn translation, issued at most two years earlier when no validity is stated, authorizing the applicant to represent and market the product in Brazil;
(III) a copy of the SBAC conformity certificate, only for devices with compulsory certification listed by ANVISA;
(IV) proof of compliance with the legal provisions of technical regulations for specific devices. The declaration must state the corporate name and full address of the legal manufacturer and the applicant, the express authorization, and a statement of knowledge and compliance with the GMP of RDC 665/2022 (or its replacement).

To petition revalidation the applicant pays the fee and files:
(I) for imported devices, the manufacturer's authorization declaration (consularized or apostilled, language and two-year rules as above, with the same content including the GMP statement);
(II) a valid ANVISA GMP certificate. The request must be filed within the period of RDC 250/2004 (§2); the GMP certificate request protocol is accepted for filing and analysis (§3). Products under notification are exempt from revalidation (Art. 28). Registration is valid for 10 years from DOU publication (Art. 11).
